it will take a while to clear up but the  debris from last week's unrest is slowly  being removed even with a shoot to kill  policy still in place after nationwide  protests turned extremely violent in  kazakhstan's largest city  we passed boarded up shops where looters  had run riot but the traffic's in full  flow and the internet's back on a game  changer after six days absence from the  world wide web  we didn't leave the house only yesterday  and today  we are slowly getting back on our feet  ten thousand people have been detained  one of them a well-known musician from  neighboring kyrgyzstan was released on  monday at the request of kurdish  authorities clearly having been beaten  i heard from inmates that if you confess  that you were offered money and came to  almaty for the protests you will  immediately be deported to shike  it was a way home so i decided to  implicate myself  even though i didn't do it  it begs the question whether the  president's claim that this was a coup  pulled off by international terrorists  wasn't just a way to justify calling on  russia for help  this all happened extremely fast from  the way the protests spread like  wildfire across the whole country to the  sudden eruption of violence here in  almaty to the fact that president takaya  have managed to put a lid on things  essentially overnight by calling in the  assistance of russian peacekeeping  forces to help him restore law and order  now he says their main mission is  complete and that they'll start leaving  within the next couple of days  president takayev also blamed his  predecessor for a system where the rich  got richer at the people's expense music  to the ears of two homeless men that we  meet  there are no properly paid jobs but  there are jobs which pay pennies i have  10 10 gay in my pocket i want to eat and  i have no place to sleep  the sense of emergency is ebbing away  but there are deep-rooted problems still  to solve which foreign forces can't help  with  di